On average, US workers spend about 10 of their workday surfing the internet,

159
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:53,840
emailing friends or shopping online.

160
00:14:54,720 --> 00:15:00,920
This behaviour, called cyberloathing, is believed to cost employees up to $85

161
00:15:00,920 --> 00:15:03,880
billion a year in lost productivity.

162
00:15:04,680 --> 00:15:10,560
The majority of cyberloathing research focuses on ways to prevent employees from

163
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:16,000
engaging in this behaviour through interventions such as internet monitoring and

164
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:17,520
computer use policies.

165
00:15:18,540 --> 00:15:24,880
But it turns out such behaviour may not be a sign that a worker is lazy or just

166
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:31,100
wasting time. Social media use at work has been linked to higher levels of employee

167
00:15:31,100 --> 00:15:33,840
engagement and job satisfaction.

168
00:15:34,740 --> 00:15:40,640
New research suggests cyberloathing can help workers cope with an exceptionally

169
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:42,320
stressful work environment.

170
00:15:43,260 --> 00:15:48,800
But is cyberloathing actually effective at reducing employee stress levels?

171
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:53,980
That's the question my research team wanted to answer in our new study.

172
00:15:53,980 --> 00:16:02,020
Our hypothesis was that cyberloathing may serve as a mini break, giving employees an

173
00:16:02,020 --> 00:16:05,280
opportunity to recover from stressful work situations.

174
00:16:05,940 --> 00:16:12,000
To test this, we asked workers to complete an online survey and rank how much time

175
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:12,990
they spent cyberloathing,

176
00:16:13,980 --> 00:16:16,560
checking non -work emails and shopping.

177
00:16:17,020 --> 00:16:23,600
We also asked them to rank job satisfaction, their desire to quit and how often

178
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:29,300
they've experienced mistreatment at work, such as being bullied, threatened or

179
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:36,880
yelled at. As you might expect, found that overall more mistreatment at was

180
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:43,060
correlated with lower levels of job satisfaction and a greater desire to quit.

181
00:16:43,060 --> 00:16:50,480
More interestingly, found that cyberloathing effectively buffered this connection.

182
00:16:51,140 --> 00:16:56,940
That is, mistreated workers who spent more time surfing the and checking emails

183
00:16:56,940 --> 00:17:02,840
reported higher job satisfaction and were less likely to want to quit than similar

184
00:17:02,840 --> 00:17:06,900
participants who didn't cyberloathe as much.

185
00:17:06,900 --> 00:17:13,900
This suggests that cyberloathing acts as a sort relief valve for workers, helping

186
00:17:13,900 --> 00:17:17,220
them recover from stressful experiences.

187
00:17:18,620 --> 00:17:23,960
While we did not directly assess how cyberloathing affects worker performance, we

188
00:17:23,960 --> 00:17:30,060
believe by relieving stress it may not only reduce worker turnover but also

189
00:17:30,060 --> 00:17:31,980
ultimately bolster productivity.

190
00:17:31,980 --> 00:17:37,880
This fits with other recent research that suggests taking short breaks is associated

191
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:40,700
with higher levels of daily job performance.

192
00:17:41,880 --> 00:17:45,080
That isn't to say cyberloathing is always good.

193
00:17:45,900 --> 00:17:51,480
Too much time spent on non -work activities likely causes performance to suffer.

194
00:17:52,260 --> 00:17:56,440
All in all, managers should be more lenient with employees.

195
00:17:56,440 --> 00:18:02,280
We believe a bit of online shopping or surfing the internet at work could make

196
00:18:02,280 --> 00:18:05,400
workers more productive in the long run.

