00:19:10,320 --> 00:19:14,340
When Julius Morton moved to Nebraska City in 1854,

201
00:19:15,040 --> 00:19:18,280
he faced a problem shared by many settlers in the territory.

202
00:19:18,640 --> 00:19:20,620
It was a treeless plain.

203
00:19:21,200 --> 00:19:25,780
That meant no trees for building materials or for fuel.

204
00:19:25,780 --> 00:19:32,580
But Morton was one of the world's first conservationists, stating, prosperity we

205
00:19:32,580 --> 00:19:36,660
ought to plant as many forests as we have exhausted and consumed.

206
00:19:37,340 --> 00:19:40,740
So he started planting trees, beginning with his own land.

207
00:19:41,340 --> 00:19:46,340
By 1860, Morton possessed a forest of more than 300 trees.

208
00:19:46,820 --> 00:19:50,220
A few years later, he had more than 1,000.

209
00:19:50,220 --> 00:19:54,380
As the forests grew, so did Morton's influence in Nebraska.

210
00:19:54,380 --> 00:20:00,380
This was largely because Morton founded the Nebraska City News, the state's first

211
00:20:00,380 --> 00:20:05,800
newspaper, in which he frequently wrote editorials about the practical and aesthetic

212
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:07,400
benefits of tree planting.

213
00:20:08,420 --> 00:20:13,540
He also organized the Nebraska State Gardening Society and served on the Nebraska

214
00:20:13,540 --> 00:20:15,160
State Board of Agriculture.

215
00:20:16,180 --> 00:20:21,500
While serving on the board, Morton came up with an idea to spread his belief in tree

216
00:20:21,500 --> 00:20:26,080
planting statewide. On January 4th, 1872,

217
00:20:26,740 --> 00:20:31,900
Morton drafted a resolution that April 10th be designated Day for the Planting of

218
00:20:31,900 --> 00:20:33,700
Trees in the State of Nebraska,

219
00:20:34,800 --> 00:20:37,660
and urged people to go out and plant trees themselves.

220
00:20:38,380 --> 00:20:43,460
Morton called the special event Arbor Day, as arbor is Latin for tree.

221
00:20:43,460 --> 00:20:50,720
The state government agreed, and on April 10th, 1872, the first unofficial Arbor Day

222
00:20:50,720 --> 00:20:52,940
was celebrated throughout Nebraska.

223
00:20:53,980 --> 00:20:58,620
Prizes were awarded to counties, cities, and individuals who planted the largest

224
00:20:58,620 --> 00:20:59,600
number of trees.

225
00:21:00,420 --> 00:21:06,700
That day, an astounding one million trees were planted in Nebraska, an average of

226
00:21:06,700 --> 00:21:10,920
more than six for every man, woman, and child in the state.

227
00:21:10,920 --> 00:21:19,940
Since 1885, Nebraska has planted more than 700,000 acres of trees, earning it the

228
00:21:19,940 --> 00:21:22,300
nickname the Tree Planter's State.

229
00:21:23,360 --> 00:21:28,240
Arbor Day became a legal civic holiday in the state in 1885.

230
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:33,040
It was held on April 22nd, Julius Morton's birthday.

231
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:39,200
In addition to a parade in Nebraska City, Morton introduced what has since become a

232
00:21:39,200 --> 00:21:41,300
long -standing Arbor Day tradition.

233
00:21:42,360 --> 00:21:45,440
School children went outside and planted trees together.

234
00:21:46,380 --> 00:21:51,100
In 1970, nearly a century after Arbor Day was first celebrated,

235
00:21:51,860 --> 00:21:57,120
President Richard Nixon declared the last Friday of every April to be observed as

236
00:21:57,120 --> 00:21:58,320
National Arbor Day.

237
00:21:58,320 --> 00:22:04,620
All 50 states recognize the April observance, although many hold an additional State

238
00:22:04,620 --> 00:22:08,160
Arbor Day in a month more suited to local tree planting.

